# ~/.config/radare2/radare2rc — dotfiles/radare2/radare2rc,
# symlinked by `./sync.sh radare2`.
#
# Scaffolding for the opt-in reverse-engineering tier (`bash re.setup.sh`).
# See re/README.md; the tool inventory lives in
# agents/skills/cleanroom/reference/tool-shelf.md and only there.
#
# ⛔ DELIBERATELY EMPTY. Every line below is commented out.
#
# radare2 is NOT INSTALLED on this box yet (apt has 5.9.8). Nothing here has
# been run, so nothing here is enabled.
#
# ⚠️ THE GOTCHA THAT MATTERS
#
#   r2 reads this file on EVERY invocation, including the non-interactive one
#   that makes r2 worth having:
#
#       r2 -q -c 'aaa; afl; pdf @ main' -A binary
#
#   So an `e` setting written here for interactive comfort silently rewrites
#   the bytes a scripted parse consumes. That is the shared-failure-mode trap:
#   the analysis still "succeeds", it just reports something else. Put display
#   preferences in the -c command line of the run that wants them, not here,
#   unless you have measured the effect on the scripted output too.
#
#   (`-N` skips this file entirely — the escape hatch when a scripted run must
#   be independent of user config. Consider using it in any harness.)

# --- deterministic, diffable output ----------------------------------------
# These are the settings most likely to be RIGHT for agent use — colour codes
# and box-drawing characters are noise in a diff — and they are still off,
# because "likely right" is not "measured here". Enable them per-run first:
#   r2 -e scr.color=0 -q -c '...' binary
# e scr.color=0
# e scr.utf8=false
# e scr.interactive=false

# --- analysis depth ---------------------------------------------------------
# `aaa` is the usual default; `aaaa` is much slower and occasionally worse.
# Pin the depth in the command line of the run so the finding records which
# analysis produced it.
# e anal.depth=64

# --- what does NOT belong here ---------------------------------------------
# Per-target scripts. r2 will happily `. script.r2` from here and then every
# unrelated binary you open gets that project's assumptions applied to it.
